    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d546">

                <pb facs="0526" n="514" xml:id="pg514"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">870.811/210: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Rumania</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gunther</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bucharest</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-09-20T12:00:00+02:00">September 20,
                            1940—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received September 21—1:28 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>550. A high official of the Foreign Office has informed me in confidence that the
                    Russian Government in connection with its move to be included in the European
                    Danube Commission has insisted: 1) that Italy be excluded; 2) that Russia should
                    participate from Bratislava to the Black Sea and not merely on the lower reaches
                    of the Danube from Braila down. I understand this to mean that Russia has not
                    suggested that it should become a member of the International Commission but
                    rather that the jurisdiction of the European Commission should be extended to
                    Bratislava. Considering that Russia has never before been a member of either of
                    the two Commissions, these pretensions seemed to him rather ominous.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gunther</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
